# ── COMPLEMENT CONTROLS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The other half of the guarantee: refusals alone are satisfiable by an engine
# that refuses everything, and an over-broad protected set silently converts an
# ordinary predicate into an unconditional right. Each MUST still load.
# (Runner support for :accept / :refuse is pending — see TODO.md. Until then
# these are run by the shell loop recorded in the same bullet.)
#
#   :accept   all $x: person($x) & ~home($x)    -> prisoner($x).
#   :accept   all $x: person($x) & ~choose($x)  -> prisoner($x).
#   :accept   all $x: person($x) & ~permits($x) -> prisoner($x).
#   :accept   all $x: person($x) & ~false($x)   -> prisoner($x).
#
# NOT a control, deliberately: ~travel is refused too, for a reason unrelated to
# the floor — Article 2 derives travel behind ~prisoner, so it closes its own
# negative cycle. Listing it would pass for the wrong reason and keep passing
# after the firewall broke.
#
# ── FIREWALL REFUSALS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   :refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'believe'/   ... & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).
#   :refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'meets'/     ... & ~meets($x)   -> prisoner($x).
#   :refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'eats'/      ... & ~eats($x)    -> prisoner($x).
#   :refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'secure'/    ... & ~secure($x)  -> prisoner($x).
